  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unsettled and humid through Sunday as a frontal boundary\nlingers near the region, bringing scattered to numerous showers,\nthunderstorms, and localized heavy rain.\n\nLatest surface analysis indicates a sfc cold front slowly\ndropping south of the Mason-Dixon line early this morning, with\nscattered showers across the far northern portions of the CWA.\nSfc-based and ML CAPE values are generally ~500 J/Kg but CIN is\nalso present so there has been little to no thunder over the\npast several hrs. PWAT values are elevated however, around \n1.80\" to 1.90\" over northern portions of the FA, so these \nshowers are putting down heavy rain and have been backbuilding \nso will continue to monitor the potential issuance of Flood \nAdvisories although there has been a downward trend over the\npast few hrs. The CAMs have not been handling this activity \nparticularly well thus far, so the diminishing coverage depicted\nafter sunrise is a bit uncertain. As the front sinks south \nlater today, most of the guidance shows showers/storms \nredeveloping mainly along and S of I-64 this afternoon into this\nevening. \n\nThe cold front drops south into the region late tonight, with\nthe front stalling across southern VA and NE NC Friday afternoon.\nThe 11/00z HREF PMM mostly highlights a 70% signal for 1\"/3-hr \nQPF along and south of this boundary (southern VA/interior NE \nNC, but most areas are only in the 10% prob for 3\"/3 hr, indicating\ntoo much uncertainty for any Flood Watches. Nevertheless, localized\ndownpours and some localized flash flood issues remain the primary\nhazard, focused S-SW of I-64, with a Day 1 Marginal ERO.\nDepending on how much sunshine and instability develops, there\nis a Marginal SVR threat as well for areas along and south of\nI-64 (minus the immediate coast). Shear is not overly\nimpressive, but with steep low level lapse rates, isolated\nwet microbursts can't be ruled out. Temperatures will feature a\nrelatively sharp gradient today with more sunshine through mid \naftn across the south, expect highs into the lower 90s, with \nnorthern areas in the low- mid 80s. The front continues to sink \nsouth tonight, with most of the showers/storms ending overnight.\nThere does appear to be a good signal for low stratus and \npossible some fog across the piedmont overnight. Humid with lows\nin the upper 60s to lower 70s south, with somewhat drier air to\nthe north where lows drop into the mid 60s. \n\nWidespread precipitation chances peak Saturday, as the stalled\nboundary lifts back north as a warm front, along with a more\npronounced mid-level shortwave disturbances push across the \narea. High precipitable water values, modest instability (skinny\nCAPE profile), and weak steering flow should yield efficient \nwarm rainfall processes and additional localized hydro concerns \n(supported by an area-wide WPC ERO Marginal Risk and a localized\nupgrade to Slight for portions of the I-95 corridor, including\nmetro RIC. Considerable cloud cover will keep a lid on Saturday\nhighs to the low to mid 80s N, but highs reach into the upper\n80s SE. There may even be some weak CAD, potentially keeping far\nNW zones even cooler (but still very humid). The boundary \nwashes out Sunday, leading to noticeably lower convective \ncoverage for the NW 1/2 of the area, but PoPs remain high chc to\nlikely for the SE. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Dry with seasonable temperatures early next week, with \ntemperatures warming above normal by midweek as an upper ridge \nbuilds back into the region.\n\nThe upper level flow becomes NW on Monday as a strong upper\nlevel trough dives SE from the Great Lakes and pushes off the\nNE and mid-Atlantic coast Monday night, with an upper level\nridge slowly expanding NE from the lower MS Valley. This looks\nstrong enough to drive the sfc cold front south of the CWA\nMonday, and bring significantly lower dew pts more typical of\nSeptember. Becoming mostly sunny with highs in the 80s Monday,\nfollowed by a cooler, more pleasant night Monday night/Tue AM \nwith lows mostly in the low-mid 60s with some 50s possible \nacross the piedmont and interior MD eastern shore as sfc high \npressure builds south. Diurnal shower and thunderstorm chances \ngradually tick back up mid-to-late week as heat and moisture \nstart to return as the upper level ridge expands E-NE to the TN \nValley and central/southern Appalachians.",
